            "paragraphs": [
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Key findings",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "17pt",
                      "fontWeight": "bold"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "1.  Beech was the only species clearly worse in 2026 than in the 2018 drought, and the hardest hit of the five.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "14pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "2.  Overall, trees coped no worse than in 2018: more were affected, but not more deeply. Oak did better than in 2018.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "14pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "3.  The oldest beeches (trunks of 80 cm and over) were not clearly worse than middle-sized ones.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "14pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "4.  The stress was inside the leaves: chlorophyll and moisture fell, but crowns looked healthy on the ground in late September.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "14pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
